% Semantic predicates over text, backed by a service (LE_extensions_proposal §3.6).
%
% "the best match of a text among a list is a category" cannot be decided by
% rules: its arguments are free text. The template is backed by a declared
% service ("; via service matcher"), called at run time with the known
% arguments (the text and the candidates), which fills in the unknown one
% (the category). Each distinct request is made ONCE per session and cached:
% x and y have the same description, so one call answers both. Every answer
% enters the proof attributed to the service ("..., according to service
% matcher"), so a source-scoped proof can admit or exclude it.
%
% The address "stub:matcher" is the deterministic in-process test stub (the
% LE server also serves it at /test_services/matcher). A real service is an
% http(s):// address answering the same JSON protocol, or an LLM, e.g.
% "llm:openai/gpt-oss-120b" (see semantic_llm.le).

the target language is: prolog.

the knowledge base semantic match includes these services:
    matcher at stub:matcher as a semantic matcher.

the templates are:
    the category of *an item* is *a category*.
    the description of *an item* is *a text*; undefined.
    the best match of *a text* among *a list* is *a category*; via service matcher.

the knowledge base semantic match includes:

the category of an item is a category
    if the description of the item is a text
    and the best match of the text among [fruit, vehicle, tool] is the category.

scenario one is:
    the description of x is "a red bicycle with a basket".
    the description of y is "a red bicycle with a basket".
    the description of z is "a ripe banana".
    category expects answers ["the category of x is vehicle", "the category of y is vehicle", "the category of z is fruit"].

query category is:
    the category of which item is which category.
